COURSE_DATA["sen401"]={topics:[
{id:'t1',title:'Organisational context, roles and governance in SCM',why:'Definition of SCM, its four activities and who does what: the foundation for every other question.',
cards:[
{h:'What is SCM?',b:`<div class="def"><b>Software Configuration Management</b>The discipline of identifying the configuration of a system at distinct points in time to systematically control changes and maintain integrity and traceability throughout the life cycle (SWEBOK/IEEE).</div><p><b>Configuration</b> = the functional and physical characteristics of hardware/software as set out in documentation and achieved in a product.</p><p>Core activities: <b>identification · control · status accounting · auditing</b> (plus release management and planning).</p><div class="hook">I-C-S-A: "I Can See All".</div>`},
{h:'Organisational context and roles',b:`<p>SCM interacts with QA, development, project management, testing, release and customers. It sits under project management but supports <b>software quality assurance</b>.</p><table><tr><th>Role</th><th>Duty</th></tr><tr><td>Configuration manager</td><td>Owns the SCM plan, procedures, tools</td></tr><tr><td>Change Control Board (CCB)</td><td>Approves/rejects change requests</td></tr><tr><td>Developers</td><td>Check in/out, follow branching rules</td></tr><tr><td>Librarian</td><td>Maintains the software library and baselines</td></tr><tr><td>Auditor/QA</td><td>Verifies configuration matches records</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Constraints and guidance',b:`<p><b>Constraints</b>: corporate policies, contracts, regulations (safety, finance), the life-cycle model. <b>Guidance</b>: standards such as <b>IEEE 828</b> (SCM plans), ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207, CMMI.</p><p>Why it matters: prevents lost changes, simultaneous update conflicts, "it worked yesterday" bugs, and lets you rebuild any past release.</p>`},
{h:'SCM depends on its organisation',b:`<p>SCM is <b>more than a toolset</b>. It sits alongside development, QA and project management, and is shaped by:</p><ul><li>team size and structure</li><li>process model (agile, waterfall, DevOps)</li><li>criticality of the system</li><li>existing tooling and infrastructure</li></ul><p>A 5-person startup and a 200-engineer regulated enterprise both need SCM, but formality and tooling differ. Design SCM to fit the organisation, not fight it.</p>`},
{h:'Two organisational models',b:`<table><tr><th>Centralised SCM</th><th>Embedded / DevOps</th></tr><tr><td>A dedicated SCM/release team owns tooling, branching policy and the change process for the whole organisation</td><td>SCM is built into each product team's daily work; engineers own pipelines and branching, guided by shared standards</td></tr><tr><td>Large, regulated, multi-team organisations needing consistency and audit trails</td><td>Agile and DevOps-native organisations</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Where constraints come from',b:`<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td><b>External</b></td><td>Industry standards (ISO/IEC, IEEE), regulation and compliance, client contracts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Internal</b></td><td>Company tooling standards, security and data-protection policy, legacy infrastructure</td></tr><tr><td><b>Project-level</b></td><td>Team size and skills, timeline and budget, criticality and risk tolerance</td></tr></table><p><b>Guidance documents:</b> (1) SCM policy · (2) SCMP · (3) branching and coding standards · (4) Definition of Done / release checklist. They give the CCB something concrete to measure every change against.</p>`},
{h:'Roles on the slides',b:`<ul><li><b>Configuration Manager</b>: plans SCM, sets up and maintains the software library and baselines, reports configuration status.</li><li><b>CCB</b>: a small group (technical leads, QA, product/project management) that <b>approves, rejects or defers</b> changes.</li><li><b>Development team</b>: raises change requests, implements approved changes, follows the branching workflow.</li><li><b>QA/Test</b>: verifies a change works before release approval.</li><li><b>Release/DevOps engineer</b>: packages, deploys and monitors changes in CI/CD.</li><li><b>Project manager</b>: balances scope, schedule and risk.</li></ul><p>Together they turn "anyone can change anything" into a controlled, auditable process.</p>`},
{h:'Who does what in each SCM activity',b:`<table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Owners</th></tr><tr><td>Identification</td><td>Configuration Manager (leads); development team (proposes CIs)</td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>CCB (approves); development team (implements)</td></tr><tr><td>Status accounting</td><td>Configuration Manager (leads); automated CI/CD tooling</td></tr><tr><td>Audit</td><td>Configuration Manager (leads); QA or an independent auditor</td></tr></table><p><b>Case study:</b> a fintech developer wants to merge an untested demo fix straight to production. The CCB and QA should say no: the change is unapproved and unverified. A startup might allow a faster path; a regulated bank would not.</p>`}],
recall:[['Define SCM.','Identifying the configuration at distinct points in time to systematically control changes and maintain integrity and traceability through the life cycle.'],['Four core SCM activities.','Identification, control, status accounting, auditing.'],['Who approves change requests?','The Change Control Board (CCB).'],['Which IEEE standard covers SCM plans?','IEEE 828.'],['Name three problems SCM prevents.','Lost changes, simultaneous update conflicts, inability to reproduce a past release.'],['Four factors that shape SCM in an organisation.','Team size and structure, process model, system criticality, existing tooling and infrastructure.'],['Centralised vs embedded SCM?','Centralised: a dedicated SCM/release team owns tooling and change process (large, regulated). Embedded/DevOps: each product team owns its pipelines under shared standards (agile).'],['Three levels of SCM constraints with an example each.','External (ISO/IEEE standards, regulation), internal (security policy, legacy systems), project-level (team size, budget, risk tolerance).'],['Four guidance documents for SCM.','SCM policy, SCMP, branching and coding standards, Definition of Done / release checklist.'],['What can the CCB decide on a change?','Approve, reject or defer it.'],['Who leads identification, status accounting and audit?','The Configuration Manager.'],['Who implements approved changes?','The development team.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which is NOT a core SCM activity?',o:['Configuration identification','Configuration control','Configuration marketing','Configuration auditing'],a:2},{k:'gap',q:'The body that approves or rejects changes is the Change Control ___.',a:'board'},{k:'tf',q:'SCM supports software quality assurance.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'IEEE 828 is the standard for…',o:['Unit testing','SCM plans','Requirements','Maintenance'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'A dedicated release team owning branching policy for the whole company is…',o:['Embedded SCM','Centralised SCM','DevOps SCM','Ad hoc SCM'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Regulatory compliance requirements are which level of constraint?',o:['External','Internal','Project-level','Personal'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'Team size and risk tolerance are…',o:['External constraints','Internal constraints','Project-level constraints','Guidance documents'],a:2},{k:'gap',q:'The CCB can approve, reject or ___ a change.',a:'defer'},{k:'mcq',q:'Who packages, deploys and monitors approved changes in CI/CD?',o:['CCB','Release/DevOps engineer','QA','Project manager'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Only large regulated enterprises need SCM.',a:false,w:'Startups need it too; only the formality differs.'},{k:'mcq',q:'In status accounting, who works alongside the Configuration Manager?',o:['The CCB','Automated CI/CD tooling','The customer','The project manager'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Define software configuration management, explain its core activities and the roles involved.',pts:['Definition','Identification','Control','Status accounting','Auditing','Roles (CCB, CM, librarian, developers)','Why it matters'],model:`<p>SCM identifies the configuration at points in time and controls change to keep integrity and traceability. Identification names and baselines items; control evaluates and approves change; status accounting records and reports; auditing verifies the product matches its records. The configuration manager owns the plan, the CCB approves changes, the librarian keeps baselines, developers follow procedures and QA audits.</p>`}]},
{id:'t2',title:'Planning for SCM: the SCMP',why:'What goes in a Software Configuration Management Plan.',
cards:[
{h:'The SCMP',b:`<div class="def"><b>SCM Plan (SCMP)</b>A document defining the SCM activities, responsibilities, schedules, resources and procedures for a project (IEEE 828).</div><p>Planning considers: organisation and responsibilities · resources and schedule · <b>tool selection</b> · vendor/subcontractor control · interface control.</p>`},
{h:'Contents',b:`<ol><li>Introduction (purpose, scope, terms)</li><li>SCM management (organisation, responsibilities, policies)</li><li>SCM activities (identification, control, status accounting, audits/reviews, release)</li><li>Schedules</li><li>Resources (tools, people, training)</li><li>Plan maintenance</li></ol><p>Tools: version control (Git), issue tracking (Jira), build/CI (Jenkins, GitHub Actions), artifact repositories.</p>`},
{h:'SCM planning: objectives and scope',b:`<div class="def"><b>SCM planning</b>Deciding, in writing and before work starts, how configuration management will be carried out on a specific project: who does what, with which tools, to what standard. Its output is the <b>SCMP</b>.</div><p><b>Objectives</b> (what the plan must achieve): identify what is controlled, define the SCM processes, assign clear responsibility.</p><p><b>Scope</b> (what it covers): which products, life-cycle phases, teams, environments and tools. Too broad = unusable; too narrow = things slip through.</p>`},
{h:'The lecturer\'s six SCMP sections',b:`<ol><li><b>Introduction</b>: purpose, scope, definitions</li><li><b>SCM Management</b>: organisation, roles, responsibilities, policies</li><li><b>SCM Activities</b>: identification, control, status accounting, audits and reviews</li><li><b>Tools, Techniques and Environment</b>: repository, automation, infrastructure</li><li><b>Supplier/Subcontractor Control</b>: bringing external components under control</li><li><b>Records Collection and Retention</b>: baseline schedules, what is kept, where, how long</li></ol><p><b>Three pillars:</b> organisation and responsibilities · activities and procedures · schedules and resources.</p><div class="trap">The earlier card lists IEEE 828 headings (schedules, resources, plan maintenance). In this exam, use the lecturer's six.</div>`},
{h:'Good SCMP vs common pitfalls',b:`<p>A strong SCMP reads like an <b>instruction manual</b>, not a policy statement.</p><p>Vague: "changes will be managed appropriately". Concrete: "the CCB meets weekly; all merges to main require one approval".</p><table><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Too vague</td><td>Tells no one what to do</td></tr><tr><td>Written once, never updated</td><td>Drifts from reality; the SCMP is a <b>living document</b></td></tr><tr><td>Disconnected from practice</td><td>If the CCB never meets as described, the plan has failed</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['What is an SCMP?','A plan defining SCM activities, responsibilities, schedules, resources and procedures for a project.'],['Name four sections of an SCMP.','Introduction, SCM management, SCM activities, schedules, resources, plan maintenance.'],['Give three categories of SCM tool.','Version control, issue tracking, build/CI.'],['Define SCM planning (slides).','Deciding in writing, before work starts, how SCM will be done on a project: who does what, with which tools, to what standard.'],['Objectives vs scope of an SCMP?','Objectives: what the plan must achieve. Scope: what it covers (products, phases, teams, environments, tools).'],['The six SCMP sections on the slides.','Introduction; SCM management; SCM activities; tools, techniques and environment; supplier/subcontractor control; records collection and retention.'],['Three pillars of an SCMP.','Organisation and responsibilities; activities and procedures; schedules and resources.'],['Three common SCM planning pitfalls.','Too vague; written once and never updated; disconnected from practice.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Which is part of SCM planning?',o:['Tool selection','UI design','Sales forecasting','Load testing'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'The SCMP should say how subcontractor software is controlled.',a:true},{k:'gap',q:'SCMP stands for Software Configuration Management ___.',a:'plan'},{k:'mcq',q:'Which is one of the lecturer\'s six SCMP sections?',o:['Marketing plan','Supplier/subcontractor control','Risk register','Test cases'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'"Changes will be tracked appropriately" is an example of which pitfall?',o:['Too vague','Too narrow','Disconnected from practice','Over-scoped'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'The SCMP should be frozen at project kickoff.',a:false,w:'It is a living document.'},{k:'gap',q:'A strong SCMP reads like an instruction ___, not a policy statement.',a:'manual'},{k:'mcq',q:'Which section says how long baselines are kept?',o:['Introduction','SCM management','Records collection and retention','SCM activities'],a:2}],
theory:[{q:'Describe the contents of a Software Configuration Management Plan.',pts:['Definition','Introduction','Management/roles','Activities','Schedules','Resources/tools','Plan maintenance'],model:`<p>The SCMP (IEEE 828) states purpose and scope, who is responsible (CM, CCB), how each SCM activity is done, the schedule of baselines and audits, the tools and people required, and how the plan itself will be updated.</p>`}]},
{id:'t3',title:'SCM plan development and surveillance',why:'How the plan is created, approved and monitored with metrics.',
cards:[
{h:'Developing the plan',b:`<p>Assess project needs → define items and baselines → assign roles → choose tools → define procedures → review and approve → baseline the plan itself.</p><p><b>Surveillance</b> = monitoring that SCM is carried out as planned: audits, reviews, metrics.</p>`},
{h:'Measurement',b:`<p>SCM metrics: number of change requests (open/closed), average time to approve a change, number of builds and failed builds, defect trends per release, frequency of merge conflicts.</p><p>Use metrics to improve the process, not to blame individuals.</p>`},
{h:'Developing the plan: top-down meets bottom-up',b:`<p>Developing an SCMP = turning each outline bullet into a <b>testable, specific</b> commitment: named tools, named people, real dates.</p><table><tr><th>Top-down (required controls)</th><th>Bottom-up (team reality)</th></tr><tr><td>Policy and standards</td><td>Tools used daily</td></tr><tr><td>Contract and compliance</td><td>People and skills</td></tr><tr><td>Audit expectations</td><td>Workflow and cadence</td></tr></table><p>Practical test: would this draft satisfy an audit <b>and</b> guide the team's work tomorrow?</p>`},
{h:'Pre-submission checklist',b:`<ol><li><b>Named owners</b> for every activity</li><li><b>Named tools</b> (Git, GitHub, Jira, not "a repository")</li><li><b>Real cadence</b> (a CCB meeting schedule, not "as needed")</li><li><b>Testable audits</b> someone could check this week</li><li><b>No section skipped</b></li></ol><p>Practical flow: expand → swap and review → submit.</p>`},
{h:'SCM surveillance on the slides',b:`<div class="def"><b>Surveillance</b>The ongoing activity of verifying that SCM is carried out as the SCMP says: a continuous check, not a one-off audit. Run by the Configuration Manager, using evidence from existing tools (commit history, CI/CD logs, issue tracker).</div><p><b>Four everyday mechanisms:</b> CI/CD dashboards · issue-tracker audits (every merge traces to an approved ticket) · periodic spot-checks · sprint/retro review.</p>`},
{h:'Surveillance table and failure modes',b:`<table><tr><th>Control</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Failure signal</th></tr><tr><td>Authorised changes</td><td>Approved ticket linked to each merge</td><td>A merge with no approved request</td></tr><tr><td>Builds and deploys</td><td>CI/CD logs</td><td>Missing or unexplained pipeline activity</td></tr><tr><td>Baseline integrity</td><td>Release tag vs deployed version</td><td>Deployed version no longer matches the tag</td></tr><tr><td>Process adherence</td><td>Spot-checks, retrospectives</td><td>Direct pushes, bypassed approvals</td></tr></table><p><b>Three quiet failure modes:</b> shadow process (side-channel changes) · stale baselines · silent scope creep (CCB approving outside the SCMP's scope).</p><div class="hook">Surveillance is not watching the team; it compares planned controls with visible evidence.</div>`}],
recall:[['Define SCM surveillance.','Monitoring that SCM activities are performed as the plan says, through audits, reviews and metrics.'],['Name three SCM metrics.','Change request counts, change approval time, build failure rate, merge conflict frequency.'],['What two inputs must a good SCMP balance?','Top-down required controls (policy, compliance, audit) and bottom-up team reality (tools, people, workflow).'],['Five items on the pre-submission checklist.','Named owners, named tools, real cadence, testable audits, no section skipped.'],['Define SCM surveillance (slides).','The continuous activity of verifying that SCM is carried out as the SCMP says, using evidence from existing tools.'],['Four everyday surveillance mechanisms.','CI/CD dashboards, issue-tracker audits, periodic spot-checks, sprint/retro review.'],['Three signs a plan is not being followed.','Shadow process, stale baselines, silent scope creep.'],['Failure signal for baseline integrity?','The deployed version no longer matches the tagged baseline.']],
quiz:[{k:'tf',q:'Surveillance means watching developers\' screens.',a:false,w:'It means monitoring SCM compliance through audits, reviews and metrics.'},{k:'mcq',q:'Which is an SCM metric?',o:['Average change approval time','Office temperature','Number of meetings','Lines of comments'],a:0},{k:'mcq',q:'Who runs SCM surveillance?',o:['The CCB','The Configuration Manager','The customer','External auditors only'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Surveillance is a one-off audit at the end of the project.',a:false,w:'It is continuous.'},{k:'mcq',q:'A change pushed directly after a DM approval is a…',o:['Stale baseline','Shadow process','Waiver','Baseline'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Evidence that every merge traces to an approved ticket comes from…',o:['Issue-tracker audits','Release notes','Burndown charts','Unit tests'],a:0},{k:'gap',q:'When the tagged baseline no longer matches production, the baseline is ___.',a:'stale'},{k:'mcq',q:'"The CCB has an actual meeting schedule" satisfies which checklist item?',o:['Named tools','Real cadence','Named owners','Testable audits'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Explain how an SCM plan is developed and how its implementation is monitored.',pts:['Development steps','Review/approval','Surveillance defined','Audits/reviews','Metrics','Feedback/improvement'],model:`<p>Assess needs, define configuration items and baselines, assign roles, select tools, write procedures, review and approve. Surveillance uses audits, reviews and metrics such as change turnaround and build failures, and feeds lessons back into the plan.</p>`},{q:'Explain SCM surveillance. Describe four mechanisms used in practice and three signs that an SCMP is not being followed.',pts:['Definition: continuous verification against the SCMP','Owner: Configuration Manager','Evidence from existing tools','Four mechanisms','Surveillance table example (control, evidence, failure signal)','Three failure modes'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> a submitted SCMP proves a team can write a plan; surveillance proves they use it.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> surveillance is the continuous check, led by the Configuration Manager, that SCM happens as the SCMP says, using evidence already in the tools. Mechanisms: CI/CD dashboards (every build and deploy is visible); issue-tracker audits (each merge links to an approved ticket); periodic spot-checks of recent changes; sprint and retro reviews where the team flags breakdowns. Signs of failure: a <b>shadow process</b> (changes through side channels), <b>stale baselines</b> (deployed version differs from the tag) and <b>silent scope creep</b> (the CCB approves changes outside the plan's scope).</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> on the reading-assignment app, the Configuration Manager checks weekly that each merge to main has a Jira ticket approved by the CCB; a merge without one is the failure signal.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> each control is paired with evidence, an owner, a cadence and a warning sign before monitoring begins.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> surveillance compares planned controls with visible evidence and acts when they diverge; it is not about watching people.</p>`}]},
{id:'t4',title:'Configuration identification and the software library',why:'Configuration items, baselines and versioning: definitions examiners love.',
cards:[
{h:'Configuration items and baselines',b:`<div class="def"><b>Configuration item (CI)</b>An aggregation of hardware/software designated for configuration management and treated as a single entity: source code, requirements, designs, test plans, build scripts, documentation, tools.</div><div class="def"><b>Baseline</b>A formally reviewed and agreed version of a CI that serves as the basis for further development and can be changed only through formal change control.</div><p>Baselines: <b>functional</b> (requirements) → <b>allocated</b> (design) → <b>developmental</b> → <b>product</b> (release).</p>`},
{h:'Versions, revisions, variants',b:`<p><b>Version</b> = an instance of an item. <b>Revision</b> = a new version meant to replace the old one (bug fix). <b>Variant</b> = an alternative version coexisting (Windows vs Linux build). Semantic versioning MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.</p><p><b>Software library</b>: controlled collection of software and documentation. Kinds: <b>working/development</b> library → <b>controlled/master</b> library → <b>static/archive</b> library (released baselines).</p>`},
{h:'What counts as a CI (slides)',b:`<div class="def"><b>Configuration item (slides)</b>Anything placed under configuration control because the team needs to track its versions and changes over time. If losing it or silently changing it would break or confuse the project, it is probably a CI.</div><table><tr><th>Usually yes</th><th>Usually no</th></tr><tr><td>Source code, build/deploy scripts, infrastructure-as-code, requirements and design docs, test suites, third-party dependency versions</td><td>Generated build output, personal scratch branches, local-only config, anything fully reproducible from tracked CIs</td></tr></table><p>Rule of thumb: if it is in the repo and something depends on its exact version, it is a CI.</p>`},
{h:'CI types at a glance',b:`<table><tr><th>Source code</th><th>Documentation</th><th>Configuration</th><th>Test assets</th></tr><tr><td>Application and library code; build/deploy scripts</td><td>Requirements and design docs; the SCMP itself</td><td>Environment/IaC files; dependency version list</td><td>Test suites and fixtures; CI/CD pipeline definitions</td></tr></table><p><b>Identification pitfalls:</b> too granular (tracking temp files and IDE settings) · too coarse (one giant CI) · inconsistent naming (hurts status accounting and audits).</p>`},
{h:'The software library: three properties',b:`<ul><li><b>Single source of truth</b>: one authoritative copy of every CI, not "whoever's laptop has the latest".</li><li><b>Access control</b>: who can read, who can write, who must go through a pull request.</li><li><b>Version history</b>: a permanent record of what changed, when and by whom.</li></ul><p><b>Lab setup:</b> create the repo (pau-sen-401 org) → README and .gitignore → <b>branch protection</b> (no direct pushes to main) → invite teammates with least access → first commit and push.</p>`},
{h:'A good baseline and how to tag it',b:`<p>A good baseline is <b>reviewed</b> before it is set, <b>stable</b> (nothing half-finished), <b>reproducible</b> from the repository alone, and <b>clearly tagged</b>. It is a fixed point that changes move forward from, never silently backward.</p><ol><li>Commit is reviewed and merged to main</li><li><code>git tag -a v1.0 -m "message"</code> (annotated: who, when, why)</li><li><code>git push --tags</code> (publish to the team)</li><li>Record it in the baseline log (for status accounting)</li></ol><div class="hook">If you can't reproduce it from the tag alone, it isn't really a baseline.</div>`}],
recall:[['Define a configuration item.','An aggregation of software/hardware designated for CM and treated as one entity.'],['Define a baseline.','A formally reviewed and agreed version that is the basis for further work and changes only through change control.'],['Revision vs variant?','A revision replaces an earlier version; a variant coexists as an alternative.'],['Three types of software library.','Working (development), controlled (master), static (archive).'],['In 2.4.1, what does 4 mean?','Minor version: backward-compatible feature addition.'],['Slide rule of thumb for a CI.','If it is in the repository and something depends on its exact version, it is a CI.'],['Give three things that are usually NOT CIs.','Generated build output, personal scratch branches, local-only config, anything reproducible from tracked CIs (any three).'],['Three properties of a software library.','Single source of truth, access control, version history.'],['Four qualities of a good baseline.','Reviewed, stable, reproducible from the repo alone, clearly tagged.'],['Git commands to tag and share a baseline.','git tag -a v1.0 -m "message", then git push --tags.'],['Three configuration identification pitfalls.','Too granular, too coarse, inconsistent naming.'],['What does branch protection on main enforce?','No direct pushes: every change must come through a pull request.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'An Android build and an iOS build of the same release are…',o:['Revisions','Variants','Baselines','Branches only'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'A formally agreed version changed only through change control is a ___.',a:'baseline'},{k:'tf',q:'Only source code can be a configuration item.',a:false,w:'Requirements, designs, tests, scripts and docs are CIs too.'},{k:'mcq',q:'Released baselines are kept in the…',o:['Working library','Static/archive library','Temp folder','Issue tracker'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Which is usually NOT a configuration item?',o:['Build scripts','Dependency version list','Generated build output','Test suites'],a:2},{k:'mcq',q:'Which Git command creates an annotated tag?',o:['git tag v1.0','git tag -a v1.0 -m "msg"','git push --tags','git commit -a'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Any commit on main is automatically a baseline.',a:false,w:'A baseline must be reviewed, stable, reproducible and tagged.'},{k:'mcq',q:'Tracking every temp file and IDE setting is which pitfall?',o:['Too coarse','Too granular','Inconsistent naming','Stale baseline'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'The software library is the single source of ___.',a:'truth'},{k:'mcq',q:'Is the SCMP itself a CI?',o:['No, it is only a plan','Yes, as documentation','Only after release','Only in waterfall'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'In a baseline, which happens after tagging and pushing?',o:['Delete the branch','Record it in the baseline log','Raise a change request','Run an audit'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Explain configuration identification, covering configuration items, baselines, versioning and the software library.',pts:['Identification defined','CI + examples','Baseline + types','Version/revision/variant','Semantic versioning','Library types'],model:`<p>Identification selects and names configuration items (code, requirements, tests, docs) and establishes baselines (functional, allocated, developmental, product). Versions are labelled MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH; revisions replace, variants coexist. The software library moves items from working to controlled to static archive storage.</p>`}]},
{id:'t5',title:'Configuration control I: change requests and the CCB',why:'The change-control process step by step.',
cards:[
{h:'The change process',b:`<div class="flow"><span>Change request</span><em>→</em><span>Evaluate impact</span><em>→</em><span>CCB decision</span><em>→</em><span>Implement</span><em>→</em><span>Verify</span><em>→</em><span>Baseline + record</span></div><p>A <b>change request (CR)</b> records: description, reason, originator, priority, affected items. Impact analysis covers cost, schedule, risk and affected CIs.</p>`},
{h:'CCB, deviations and waivers',b:`<p>The <b>CCB</b> may approve, reject or defer. Members: PM, CM, QA, technical leads, customer rep. Multi-level CCBs for large systems.</p><p><b>Deviation</b> = authorisation <b>before</b> production to depart from a requirement. <b>Waiver</b> = authorisation <b>after</b> production to accept an item that departs from requirements.</p><div class="trap">Deviation is before; waiver is after.</div>`},
{h:'Controlled vs uncontrolled change',b:`<table><tr><th>With change control</th><th>Without change control</th></tr><tr><td>Proposed in Jira, justified, reviewed by the CCB, approved with a ticket key before any branch is cut</td><td>Someone edits main directly because "it seemed small", with no record</td></tr><tr><td>Every change traces to a reason and an authority</td><td>Two squads silently touch the same file the same week</td></tr></table><div class="def"><b>Change request (slides)</b>A formal proposal to modify a CI that is already baselined, stating what, why and impact <b>before</b> any code is touched.</div><p>"If it isn't logged and reviewed first, it isn't change control; it's just an edit."</p>`},
{h:'Anatomy of a change request',b:`<table><tr><th>Field group</th><th>Contents</th></tr><tr><td><b>Identification</b> (what)</td><td>Affected CI/file(s); linked repo and squad</td></tr><tr><td><b>Justification</b> (why)</td><td>Bug, feature or risk; one sentence, no hedging</td></tr><tr><td><b>Impact</b></td><td>Scope and effort estimate; other squads touched?</td></tr><tr><td><b>Disposition</b></td><td>Approved/rejected; ticket key (e.g. SEN-23)</td></tr></table><p>A good CR names one CI, has a justification the CCB can evaluate, is one coherent change, and flags its own impact honestly.</p>`},
{h:'Inside the CCB review: four questions',b:`<ol><li><b>Is the CI already baselined?</b> If not, it is day-one setup, not a change.</li><li><b>Is the justification specific?</b> "It's better this way" fails.</li><li><b>What is the impact?</b> Scope, risk, whose work it touches.</li><li><b>Approve, reject or request more info.</b> A ticket key is issued only once the CCB is satisfied.</li></ol><p>In this course <b>Jira</b> is the system of record; the ticket key is your authorisation.</p>`},
{h:'Raising a CR and the pitfalls',b:`<p><b>Lab steps:</b> pick a task from the squad's task bank → squad lead opens a Jira issue of type "Change Request" linked to the repo → describe what, why, impact → submit to the CCB (lecturer + TA, live) → receive the ticket key.</p><table><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Vague justification</td><td>"Clean-up" tells the CCB nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Skipping the queue</td><td>Starting work before a ticket key exists</td></tr><tr><td>No impact assessment</td><td>Not checking who else touches that file</td></tr></table>`}],
recall:[['Steps of the change-control process.','Request, impact evaluation, CCB decision, implementation, verification, baseline and record.'],['Three possible CCB decisions.','Approve, reject, defer.'],['Deviation vs waiver?','Deviation: authorised before production. Waiver: accepted after production.'],['What does a change request record?','Description, reason, originator, priority, affected items.'],['Define a change request (slides).','A formal proposal to modify an already-baselined CI, stating what, why and impact before any code is touched.'],['Four field groups of a change request.','Identification, justification, impact, disposition.'],['The CCB\'s four questions.','Is the CI baselined? Is the justification specific? What is the impact? Approve, reject or request more info.'],['What is the system of record for change requests in the course?','Jira; the ticket key (e.g. SEN-23) is the authorisation.'],['Three change control pitfalls.','Vague justification, skipping the queue, no impact assessment.'],['If a CI is not yet baselined, is editing it a change?','No. It is day-one setup, not a change.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Accepting a delivered module that misses a requirement is a…',o:['Deviation','Waiver','Baseline','Variant'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Impact analysis happens before the CCB decides.',a:true},{k:'gap',q:'A formal record asking for a change is a change ___.',a:'request'},{k:'mcq',q:'A change request is raised…',o:['After the code is written','Before any code is touched','Only at release','Only for bugs'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'"Clean-up" as a CR justification is which pitfall?',o:['Skipping the queue','Vague justification','No impact assessment','Too granular'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'The ticket key (e.g. SEN-23) belongs to which CR field group?',o:['Identification','Justification','Impact','Disposition'],a:3},{k:'tf',q:'Work may start before the ticket key is issued if the change is small.',a:false,w:'That is "skipping the queue": there is no authorisation.'},{k:'gap',q:'A deviation is authorised before production; a ___ is authorised after.',a:'waiver'},{k:'mcq',q:'Which is NOT one of the CCB\'s four questions?',o:['Is the CI baselined?','Is the justification specific?','Who wrote the original code?','What is the impact?'],a:2}],
theory:[{q:'A bank\'s customers request a new transfer-limit feature in a released mobile app. Explain how configuration control handles it.',pts:['CR raised','Impact analysis','CCB decision','Implement on branch','Test/verify','New baseline + status accounting','Structure: background → solution → scenario → combine → conclusion'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> configuration control manages change to baselined items. <b>Answer:</b> raise a CR; analyse impact on code, security, tests and schedule; the CCB approves; developers implement on a branch; QA verifies; a new baseline is released and recorded. <b>Scenario:</b> the transfer-limit CR touches the API, the app and compliance rules, so the CCB includes the compliance officer. <b>Combine:</b> the process ensures the bank can trace who approved the change and roll back. <b>Conclusion:</b> control keeps change deliberate and traceable.</p>`}]},
{id:'t6',title:'Configuration control II: branches, pull requests, merge conflicts',why:'Practical Git concepts likely in objective questions.',
cards:[
{h:'Branching',b:`<p><b>Branch</b> = an independent line of development. <b>Trunk/main</b> = mainline. Strategies: <b>feature branches</b>, <b>release branches</b>, <b>hotfix branches</b>, GitFlow, <b>trunk-based development</b> (short-lived branches, frequent merges).</p><p><b>Check-out/check-in</b> (lock-modify-unlock) vs <b>copy-modify-merge</b> (Git).</p>`},
{h:'Pull requests and conflicts',b:`<p><b>Pull request</b> = a request to merge a branch, enabling code review and CI checks before merge.</p><p><b>Merge conflict</b>: two branches change the same lines differently. Git marks <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>; a developer resolves, commits.</p><p>Reduce conflicts: small frequent merges, pull main often, clear ownership.</p>`},
{h:'Feature branches done right',b:`<p>A feature branch is an isolated copy of main, cut once the CR is approved, named <code>feature/&lt;username&gt;/&lt;ticket&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code> so it traces to its authorisation. One ticket, one branch.</p><table><tr><th>Disciplined</th><th>Undisciplined</th></tr><tr><td>One ticket, descriptive slug, merged within days, rebased on main along the way</td><td>Outlives its ticket, picks up unrelated fixes, drifts weeks behind main</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'Anatomy of a pull request',b:`<ul><li><b>Reference</b>: title and description link to the ticket (SEN-23).</li><li><b>Diff</b>: scoped to exactly what the ticket described.</li><li><b>Checks</b>: required <b>CODEOWNERS</b> approval plus a passing CI run; branch protection blocks the merge otherwise.</li></ul><p>Steps: branch from main → implement → push and open PR → request CODEOWNERS review → merge once CI passes.</p><p><b>PR checklist:</b> branch cut from latest main · title references ticket · diff scoped to ticket · CODEOWNERS approval · no unresolved comments · CI passing · no conflicts with main.</p>`},
{h:'Resolving the CHANGELOG conflict',b:`<p>Every squad edits the same CHANGELOG.md line on purpose: conflicts are routine on shared repos.</p><ol><li>Pull main and merge it into your branch (<code>git pull origin main</code>)</li><li>Git marks both entries between &lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</li><li><b>Talk to the other author</b>; agree a combined entry</li><li>Resolve, commit the merge, push</li></ol><p><b>Pitfalls:</b> giant PRs · <b>force-pushing</b> shared history (erases teammates' commits) · merging without review.</p><div class="hook">A merge conflict is Git asking a question, not a sign of failure.</div>`}],
recall:[['Define a branch.','An independent line of development diverging from the mainline.'],['What is a pull request?','A request to merge a branch, used for review and automated checks.'],['What causes a merge conflict?','Two branches change the same lines (or one deletes what another edits) differently.'],['Two ways to reduce merge conflicts.','Merge small changes often; pull from main frequently.'],['Feature branch naming pattern in the course.','feature/<username>/<ticket>-<slug>.'],['Three parts of a pull request.','Reference (ticket key), diff (scoped to the ticket), checks (CODEOWNERS approval + passing CI).'],['What does CODEOWNERS enforce?','The owner of a path must approve a PR that touches it before it can merge.'],['Four steps to resolve a merge conflict (slides).','Pull and merge main; Git flags the conflict; talk to the other author; resolve, commit, push.'],['Why is force-pushing shared history a pitfall?','It rewrites history and erases teammates\' commits.'],['Three branching/PR pitfalls.','Giant PRs, force-pushing history, merging without review.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'An urgent production fix is made on a…',o:['Feature branch','Hotfix branch','Release branch','Archive'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'Short-lived branches merged daily into main is ___-based development.',a:'trunk'},{k:'tf',q:'Git uses a copy-modify-merge model.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'Which branch name follows the course convention?',o:['fix-stuff','feature/ada/SEN-23-add-login','main2','ada-branch'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Branch protection blocks a merge without…',o:['A CODEOWNERS approval and passing CI','A release tag','A waiver','A status report'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'When a merge conflict appears, you should pick your own version and force-push.',a:false,w:'Resolve it with the other author; never force-push shared history.'},{k:'mcq',q:'Bundling several tickets into one PR is the pitfall of…',o:['Giant PRs','Stale baselines','Shadow process','Too coarse CIs'],a:0},{k:'gap',q:'A merge conflict is Git asking a ___.',a:'question'},{k:'mcq',q:'A disciplined branch is merged…',o:['Within days','After the semester','Only at release','Never'],a:0}],
theory:[{q:'Explain branching, pull requests and merge conflicts, and how a team should resolve a conflict.',pts:['Branch','Strategies','PR + review/CI','Conflict cause','Markers','Resolution steps','Prevention'],model:`<p>Branches isolate work (feature, release, hotfix). A PR proposes a merge and triggers review and CI. A conflict occurs when both branches edit the same lines; the developer opens the file, chooses or combines the changes between the markers, tests, commits and completes the merge. Small frequent merges prevent most conflicts.</p>`}]},
{id:'t7',title:'Configuration status accounting',why:'Recording and reporting: what information, what reports.',
cards:[
{h:'Status accounting',b:`<div class="def"><b>Configuration status accounting (CSA)</b>The recording and reporting of information needed to manage a configuration effectively: approved identification, status of proposed changes and implementation status of approved changes.</div><p>Answers: what changed, when, by whom, why, and what is in each release?</p>`},
{h:'Reports',b:`<p>Change request logs · baseline status reports · <b>release notes</b> · version description documents · audit trails · traceability matrices.</p><p>Tools: Git log, Jira dashboards, build dashboards.</p>`},
{h:'CSA: reading the record, not re-investigating',b:`<div class="def"><b>CSA (slides)</b>The recorded, reportable state of every configuration item and change request: a read of what identification and control already produced, not a fresh investigation.</div><p>Anyone (lecturer, CCB, new teammate) can ask "where do things stand?" without searching chat history.</p><table><tr><th>Panel-based</th><th>Manual chasing</th></tr><tr><td>Read Jira's Development panel once; anyone can reproduce the report</td><td>Ping each squad lead; piece answers from memory; no two reports count the same way</td></tr></table><div class="hook">If you have to ask someone to find out, the record isn't doing its job.</div>`},
{h:'The Jira Development panel',b:`<table><tr><th>Panel shows</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Report effect</th></tr><tr><td>Branch</td><td>Linked feature branch, existing or merged</td><td>Blank = Not Started</td></tr><tr><td>Pull request</td><td>Open, merged or absent; CODEOWNERS satisfied?</td><td>Merged = Closed</td></tr><tr><td>Build</td><td>Latest CI result: pass, fail, not run</td><td>Feeds pass rate</td></tr></table><p><b>Worked example:</b> SEN-31 opens with no branch → Not Started; feature/jane/SEN-31-fix appears → In Progress; PR merges → Closed; latest CI run becomes its build status.</p>`},
{h:'Compiling a status report',b:`<ol><li>Filter Jira to your squad's tickets this cycle</li><li>Read each ticket's Development panel</li><li>Tally open vs closed (closed <b>only once the PR has merged</b>)</li><li><b>Build pass rate</b> = passing runs ÷ total runs</li><li>Count commits this cycle across linked branches</li></ol><p><b>Pitfalls:</b> reporting guesses · double-counting (closing before merge) · stale snapshots (reusing last week's numbers).</p><div class="hook">An honest "still open" beats an optimistic "nearly there".</div>`}],
recall:[['Define status accounting.','Recording and reporting configuration information: identification, proposed changes and implementation status.'],['Name three CSA reports.','Change logs, baseline status reports, release notes / version description documents.'],['Define CSA (slides).','The recorded, reportable state of every CI and change request: a read of what identification and control already produced.'],['What three things does Jira\'s Development panel show per ticket?','Linked branch, pull request status, latest build (CI) result.'],['When does a ticket count as closed?','Only once its PR has merged.'],['Formula for build pass rate.','Passing runs ÷ total runs across the squad\'s branches.'],['Three status-reporting pitfalls.','Reporting guesses, double-counting, stale snapshots.'],['A ticket with no linked branch is reported as…','Not Started.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'"Which bugs were fixed in v3.2?" is answered by…',o:['Status accounting','Identification','Auditing','Planning'],a:0},{k:'tf',q:'Release notes are a status accounting output.',a:true},{k:'mcq',q:'A ticket counts as closed when…',o:['The developer says it is done','Its PR has merged','The branch is created','The CCB approves it'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Reusing last week\'s numbers in this week\'s report is…',o:['Double-counting','A stale snapshot','Reporting guesses','A shadow process'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'Status accounting should re-investigate each change from scratch.',a:false,w:'It reads what identification and control already recorded.'},{k:'gap',q:'Build pass rate = passing runs ÷ ___ runs.',a:'total'},{k:'mcq',q:'In Jira, the tool that links each ticket to its branch, PR and build is the…',o:['Backlog','Development panel','Roadmap','Sprint board'],a:1}],
theory:[{q:'Explain configuration status accounting and the information it provides.',pts:['Definition','Questions answered','Reports','Tools','Value to management'],model:`<p>CSA records and reports configuration information so managers know the state of every item and change: what changed, when, why and by whom. Outputs include change logs, baseline reports, release notes and audit trails, drawn from Git and issue trackers.</p>`}]},
{id:'t8',title:'Configuration auditing',why:'FCA vs PCA: a classic "distinguish" question.',
cards:[
{h:'Audits',b:`<div class="def"><b>Configuration audit</b>An independent examination to check that the product conforms to requirements and that the configuration records match the product.</div><table><tr><th>Functional Configuration Audit (FCA)</th><th>Physical Configuration Audit (PCA)</th></tr><tr><td>Does the item <b>perform</b> as its requirements specify? (test results)</td><td>Does the built item <b>match its documentation</b>? (design docs, version list)</td></tr></table><p>In-process audits check SCM procedures during development.</p>`},
{h:'Audits on the slides: record vs reality',b:`<div class="def"><b>Configuration audit (slides)</b>Verifies that what is documented (the SCMP, CODEOWNERS, the branch convention) still matches what exists in the repo and build.</div><table><tr><th>Functional audit</th><th>Physical audit</th></tr><tr><td>Does it <b>work</b>? Feature tested against the ticket's acceptance criteria, not just merged</td><td>Is the <b>record true</b>? Branch names, CODEOWNERS and main's build status match the documentation</td></tr></table><div class="hook">Functional asks if it works; physical asks if the record matches reality.</div>`},
{h:'What the squad audit checks',b:`<table><tr><th>Row</th><th>Question</th><th>Followed if</th></tr><tr><td>Naming</td><td>Do branches follow feature/&lt;user&gt;/&lt;ticket&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;?</td><td>All match; exceptions named</td></tr><tr><td>CODEOWNERS</td><td>Does it point to the right owners?</td><td>Updated after any squad reshuffle</td></tr><tr><td>Build</td><td>Does main still build and pass CI?</td><td>Latest CI run passing</td></tr></table><p>Verdict: <b>Followed / Drifted</b> per row, with one line of evidence. Worked example: branch hotfix-login breaks the convention → flagged Drifted, not deleted.</p>`},
{h:'Running the audit and its pitfalls',b:`<ol><li>Pull your Week 4 SCMP</li><li>List actual branches vs the convention</li><li>Diff CODEOWNERS against reality</li><li>Confirm main builds (latest CI run)</li><li>File the checklist with evidence</li></ol><p><b>Pitfalls:</b> auditing from memory · blaming the drift · skipping evidence.</p><p><b>Drift is normal</b> entropy, not sabotage. The audit surfaces it early while it is a one-line fix.</p>`}],
recall:[['FCA vs PCA?','FCA verifies the item performs to its requirements; PCA verifies the as-built item matches its documentation.'],['Purpose of a configuration audit.','To confirm the product conforms to requirements and records match the product.'],['Functional vs physical audit (slides).','Functional: does it work against its acceptance criteria? Physical: does the record (branch names, CODEOWNERS, build status) match reality?'],['Three rows of the squad audit checklist.','Naming, CODEOWNERS, build.'],['Two possible verdicts per audit row.','Followed or Drifted, with one line of evidence.'],['Three auditing pitfalls.','Auditing from memory, blaming the drift, skipping evidence.'],['Is drift a failure?','No. It is normal entropy; finding it means the audit is working.']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Checking that the release contains exactly the listed file versions is a…',o:['FCA','PCA','CSA','CR'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'FCA stands for Functional Configuration ___.',a:'audit'},{k:'mcq',q:'"Does main\'s CODEOWNERS file name the right owners?" is part of a…',o:['Functional audit','Physical audit','Status report','Change request'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Testing a feature against its acceptance criteria is a…',o:['Physical audit','Functional audit','Spot-check','Baseline'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'A branch that breaks the naming convention should be deleted during the audit.',a:false,w:'It is flagged Drifted, not deleted.'},{k:'mcq',q:'Reporting what the SCMP said without opening the repo is…',o:['Skipping evidence','Auditing from memory','Blaming the drift','A shadow process'],a:1},{k:'gap',q:'Each audit row is marked Followed or ___.',a:'drifted'}],
theory:[{q:'Distinguish functional and physical configuration audits with examples.',pts:['Audit defined','FCA','FCA example','PCA','PCA example','When performed'],model:`<p>FCA confirms the software meets its functional requirements, e.g. reviewing test results for each requirement. PCA confirms the delivered build matches its design documents and version description, e.g. comparing release files to the manifest. Both precede a product baseline.</p>`}]},
{id:'t9',title:'Software maintenance: categories, cost and estimation',why:'Four maintenance types, Lehman\'s laws and cost estimation calculations.',
cards:[
{h:'Categories',b:`<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><b>Corrective</b></td><td>Fix faults</td><td>Fix crash on login</td></tr><tr><td><b>Adaptive</b></td><td>Adapt to a changed environment</td><td>Support new Android version</td></tr><tr><td><b>Perfective</b></td><td>Improve performance or add features</td><td>Faster search</td></tr><tr><td><b>Preventive</b></td><td>Improve maintainability, prevent future problems</td><td>Refactor, update docs</td></tr></table><p>Perfective + adaptive usually take the largest share of effort; maintenance is often 60–80% of life-cycle cost.</p>`},
{h:'Why it is costly; Lehman',b:`<p>Cost factors: team stability, contract responsibility, staff skills, program age and structure, documentation quality.</p><p><b>Lehman's laws</b>: continuing change (a system must change or become less useful) · increasing complexity (unless work is done to reduce it) · and more.</p>`},
{h:'Estimation',b:`<p><b>Annual Change Traffic (ACT)</b> = fraction of the code changed per year. <b>Annual maintenance effort</b> = ACT × development effort. Example: development 120 person-months, ACT 0.15 → 18 PM/yr.</p><p>COCOMO-style adjustment multiplies by an effort adjustment factor (EAF): 18 × 1.1 = 19.8 PM.</p>`},
{h:'Maintenance on the slides',b:`<div class="def"><b>Software maintenance (slides)</b>Any change made to a system after its initial release: fixing bugs, adapting to a new OS or dependency, improving performance, or preventing future failures.</div><p>Industry studies put maintenance at <b>well over half</b> of lifecycle cost. "If you only budget for building it, you've only budgeted for the smaller half."</p><table><tr><th>Reactive</th><th>Proactive</th></tr><tr><td><b>Corrective</b>: bug users already hit · <b>Adaptive</b>: environment changed (new OS, dependency, regulation)</td><td><b>Perfective</b>: improve what already works (performance, readability, cleaner API) · <b>Preventive</b>: restructure now so a future failure never happens</td></tr></table>`},
{h:'What goes into an estimate',b:`<table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Rule</th></tr><tr><td><b>Effort</b></td><td>Engineer-days, from code you've read, not the ticket title</td></tr><tr><td><b>Risk</b></td><td>Low/Medium/High, naming what could blow it up</td></tr><tr><td><b>Confidence</b></td><td>How sure you are; low is a valid answer</td></tr><tr><td><b>Assumption</b></td><td>The riskiest unknown, stated so it can be checked</td></tr></table><p><b>Worked example:</b> Squad A's JWT/OAuth integration touches three services. Effort 6 engineer-days; risk High (refresh-race condition); confidence Medium (third-party provider unverified).</p>`},
{h:'Estimation pitfalls and the coming weeks',b:`<p><b>Pitfalls:</b> estimating the title · hiding the risk (one confident number for a wide range) · confidence by default (marking everything High).</p><p>Textbook cases have a clean answer key; real backlog items do not.</p><p><b>Teaching plan, weeks 12–13</b> (no slides yet): maintenance process and <b>program comprehension</b> (reading unfamiliar code via git log and git blame) · re-engineering, reverse engineering, migration and retirement.</p><div class="trap">The ACT formula on the earlier card is textbook material; the slides estimate with effort, risk and confidence instead. Learn both.</div>`}],
recall:[['Four maintenance categories.','Corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive.'],['Updating an app for a new OS version is…','Adaptive maintenance.'],['Refactoring to ease future change is…','Preventive maintenance.'],['Formula for annual maintenance effort?','ACT × development effort (× EAF if given).'],['State Lehman\'s law of continuing change.','A system must continually change or become progressively less useful.'],['Define software maintenance (slides).','Any change made to a system after its initial release.'],['Which maintenance types are reactive and which proactive?','Reactive: corrective, adaptive. Proactive: perfective, preventive.'],['Four elements of a maintenance estimate (slides).','Effort, risk, confidence, assumption.'],['Unit of effort in the slide estimates.','Engineer-days.'],['Three estimation pitfalls.','Estimating the title, hiding the risk, confidence by default.'],['What share of lifecycle cost do the slides give to maintenance?','Well over half.'],['Name four week 12–13 topics in the teaching plan.','Program comprehension, re-engineering, reverse engineering, migration, retirement (any four).']],
quiz:[{k:'mcq',q:'Adding dark mode at users\' request is…',o:['Corrective','Adaptive','Perfective','Preventive'],a:2},{k:'tf',q:'Maintenance usually costs less than development over a product\'s life.',a:false},{k:'gap',q:'ACT stands for Annual Change ___.',a:'traffic'},{k:'mcq',q:'Updating an app because a regulation changed is…',o:['Corrective','Adaptive','Perfective','Preventive'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'On the slides, perfective and preventive maintenance are…',o:['Reactive','Proactive','Corrective','Emergency'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Pricing a ticket from its one-line summary is…',o:['Hiding the risk','Estimating the title','Confidence by default','Gold plating'],a:1},{k:'tf',q:'"Low confidence" is a valid part of a maintenance estimate.',a:true},{k:'gap',q:'Slide estimates measure effort in engineer-___.',a:'days'},{k:'mcq',q:'Fixing a bug users have already hit is…',o:['Preventive','Corrective','Perfective','Adaptive'],a:1},{k:'mcq',q:'Restructuring code now so a future failure never happens is…',o:['Preventive','Corrective','Adaptive','Reactive'],a:0}],
theory:[{q:'Explain the four categories of software maintenance with examples and why maintenance is expensive.',pts:['Corrective','Adaptive','Perfective','Preventive','Examples','Cost share','Cost factors / Lehman'],model:`<p>Corrective fixes faults; adaptive adjusts to environment changes; perfective improves features or performance; preventive improves maintainability. Maintenance takes most life-cycle cost because systems must keep changing (Lehman), complexity grows, original staff leave and documentation decays.</p>`},{q:'Distinguish reactive from proactive maintenance, and explain how to produce a credible maintenance estimate for a real backlog item.',pts:['Maintenance defined','Reactive: corrective + adaptive with examples','Proactive: perfective + preventive with examples','Cost: over half of lifecycle','Estimate: effort, risk, confidence, assumption','Worked example','Pitfalls'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> maintenance is any change after initial release and takes well over half of lifecycle cost.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> <b>reactive</b> maintenance responds to something that already broke or changed: corrective (a bug users hit) and adaptive (a new OS, dependency or regulation). <b>Proactive</b> maintenance acts first: perfective (better performance, cleaner API) and preventive (restructuring to avoid future failure). A credible estimate names <b>effort</b> in engineer-days from code actually read, <b>risk</b> (Low/Medium/High and what could blow it up), <b>confidence</b>, and the <b>riskiest assumption</b>.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> JWT/OAuth integration touches three services: 6 engineer-days, High risk (token-refresh race condition), Medium confidence (third-party provider untested).</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> the category tells you why the work exists; the estimate tells you what it will cost and how sure you are. Avoid estimating the title, hiding risk, or claiming high confidence by default.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> plan for maintenance as the larger half of cost, and estimate it with effort, risk and confidence, not a bare number.</p>`}]}
],
calc:[{t:'ACT',q:'Development effort 200 PM, ACT 0.12. Annual maintenance effort (PM)?',ans:24,tol:0.01,sol:'200 × 0.12 = <b>24 PM/yr</b>.'},{t:'ACT',q:'50,000 LOC system; 7,500 LOC added or modified per year. ACT?',ans:0.15,tol:0.001,sol:'7,500 ÷ 50,000 = <b>0.15</b>.'},{t:'EAF',q:'Base maintenance 30 PM, EAF 1.2. Adjusted effort?',ans:36,tol:0.01,sol:'30 × 1.2 = <b>36 PM</b>.'}]
};
